  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Explain why the hypotenuse in a right-angled triangle will always be the longest side.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "If $c$ is the hypotenuse, $c^2=a^2+b^2>a^2$ and $c^2>b^2$, so $c>a$ and $c>b$.",
      "worked": "Let the positive side lengths be $a$, $b$ and $c$, with $c$ the hypotenuse. By Pythagoras, $c^2=a^2+b^2$. Since $b^2>0$, $c^2>a^2$, so $c>a$. Similarly, since $a^2>0$, $c^2>b^2$, so $c>b$. Therefore the hypotenuse is the longest side.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 28
  },
